---
// Home (site design §6): what the Open Maid project is and its parts (site plan §11), its games and their support
// levels, where to get OME and the games' databases. It has no sidebar, so it is the phone's only way to the databases:
// the hero and the games table link each one.
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import ome from 'virtual:ome';
import { databases as listDatabases, guide } from '../lib/sections.mjs';

const releases = `${ome.repository}/releases/latest`;
const databases = listDatabases();
const databaseOf = new Map(databases.map((d) => [d.game, d]));
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{
		title: ome.title,
		template: 'splash',
		head: [{ tag: 'title', content: ome.title }],
		hero: {
			tagline: 'An open engine, modding tools and game databases for Eushully’s games.',
			actions: [
				{ text: 'Read the modding guide', link: guide.href, icon: 'right-arrow' },
				...databases.map((d) => ({ text: `The ${d.label} database`, link: d.href, icon: 'right-arrow', variant: 'secondary' })),
				{ text: 'Get OME', link: releases, icon: 'external', variant: 'minimal' },
			],
		},
	}}
>
	<h2>What it is</h2>
	<p>
		Open Maid is a project for Eushully’s games: an engine that runs them, tools to mod them, and databases of what
		they hold.
	</p>
	<p>
		Its engine, Open Maid Engine (OME), runs the games from your own copy of them. No game file is part of OME: you
		supply an installed game, and OME reads it. It runs on Windows and Linux, and it is free software under the MIT
		License.
	</p>
	<p>
		Modding comes first. A mod is an Open Maid Pack (OMP) that patches a game’s scripts, written in Open Maid Script
		(OMS), adds scenes and assets, translates the game’s text, runs Lua beside its scripts or changes its data
		tables. Script mods also export to the original engine; translations, Lua and data files run in OME.
	</p>

	<h2>The games</h2>
	<p>
		Each game has two support levels. Play says how well OME runs it, from P1 (OME reads the game) to P4 (verified
		against the original to its ending). Modding says what a modder gets, from M1 (script mods) to M3 (a base mod for
		Lua and data files). <a href="/docs/games/">The supported games</a> page explains each level.
	</p>
	<table class="ome-games">
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Game</th><th>Play</th><th>Modding</th><th>Pages</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{
				ome.games.map((game) => (
					<tr>
						<td>{game.title}</td>
						<td>{game.play ?? '—'}</td>
						<td>{game.modding ?? '—'}</td>
						<td>
							<a href={`/docs/games/${game.id}/`}>Modding guide</a>
							{databaseOf.has(game.id) && <>{' · '}<a href={databaseOf.get(game.id)!.href}>Database</a></>}
							{game.site && <>{' · '}<a href={game.site}>The maker’s site</a></>}
						</td>
					</tr>
				))
			}
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2>Get OME</h2>
	<p>
		OME’s releases are on <a href={releases}>GitHub</a>, with packages for Windows and Linux. Each carries a
		launcher, which keeps a profile for each game you play, with its mods.
	</p>

	{
		databases.length > 0 && (
			<>
				<h2>The game databases</h2>
				<p>
					A page for each record of a game’s data (an item, a character, a skill, a recipe …), read by OME from the
					game’s own data tables:{' '}
					{databases.map((d, i) => (
						<>
							{i > 0 && ', '}
							<a href={d.href}>{d.label}</a>
						</>
					))}
					.
				</p>
			</>
		)
	}
</StarlightPage>
